            title: `Q1. Define Property. Explain the kinds of property. Distinguish between tangible and intangible property. (Part A: 3m / Part B: 10m)`,
            content: `Meaning of Property

The term property has been used in the Transfer of Property Act in its widest sense. It includes every type of interest and right that has an economic value. Property is not merely the physical thing itself, but includes the bundle of rights that the law recognizes with respect to that thing. The Transfer of Property Act, 1882 does not define the word property, but Section 6 specifies that property of any kind may be transferred, except as otherwise provided.

Kinds of Property

1. Movable and Immovable Property: Immovable property, as per Section 3 of the TPA read with Section 3(26) of the General Clauses Act, includes land, benefits arising out of land, and things attached to the earth. Movable property is everything else that is not immovable. Trees standing on land, buildings, and fixtures are immovable. Timber, crops ready for harvest (standing timber), and growing crops are movable in certain contexts.

2. Tangible and Intangible Property: Tangible property is property that has a physical existence and can be touched, seen, and felt, such as land, buildings, goods, and chattels. Intangible property has no physical existence but is recognized by law, such as copyrights, patents, trademarks, goodwill, easements, and actionable claims. A debt is intangible property. A right to receive rent is intangible property.

3. Private and Public Property: Private property belongs to individuals or private entities and is subject to their exclusive use and control. Public property belongs to the State or is held for the benefit of the public, such as roads, rivers, parks, and government buildings.

CASE LAW: Shanta Bai v. State of Bombay (1958) - The Bombay High Court discussed the wide meaning of the term property and held that it includes both tangible and intangible property, and that the right to property is not limited to ownership of physical objects but extends to every possible interest recognized by law.

CASE LAW: Ananda Behera v. State of Orissa (1956) - The Supreme Court held that the right to catch and carry fish from a lake is property within the meaning of Article 19(1)(f) (now repealed) and Article 31. This shows that property extends to intangible rights and interests.

EXAM TIP: For Part A (3 marks), write the definition and list the three classifications. For Part B (10 marks), elaborate each classification with examples and cite at least one case law.`,
