            title: `Q2. Discuss the General Principles of Transfer of Property. What property can and cannot be transferred? (Part B: 10m)`,
            content: `Definition of Transfer of Property (Section 5)

Section 5 of the Transfer of Property Act defines transfer of property as an act by which a living person conveys property, in present or in future, to one or more other living persons, or to himself and one or more other living persons. The phrase 'living person' includes a company or association or body of individuals, whether incorporated or not. The transfer can be made in the present or in the future. The word 'conveys' is used in its widest sense and includes every mode of disposition.

What May Be Transferred (Section 6)

Section 6 lays down the general rule that property of any kind may be transferred. This is an enabling provision. However, the section then lists the exceptions, that is, the properties that CANNOT be transferred.

What Cannot Be Transferred (Section 6)

(a) Spes Successionis (Chance of an heir-apparent): The chance of an heir-apparent succeeding to an estate, the chance of a relation obtaining a legacy on the death of a kinsman, or any other mere possibility of a like nature, cannot be transferred. This is because the transferor has no present right; it is a mere expectancy which may never materialize. For example, a son cannot transfer his expected share in his father's property during the father's lifetime.

CASE LAW: Syed Mohd. Baksh v. Fakhr Jahan (1932) - The Privy Council held that spes successionis, being a mere possibility, is incapable of transfer. A person who may or may not inherit has no transferable interest.

(b) Right of re-entry: A right of re-entry cannot be transferred to anyone except the owner of the property affected. If a lessor has a right to re-enter the premises on breach of conditions by the lessee, this right cannot be transferred independently to a third party.

(c) Easement apart from dominant heritage: An easement is a right attached to a particular piece of land (dominant heritage). It cannot be separated and transferred independently of the dominant heritage. An easement is inseparable from the land to which it is annexed.

(d) Restricted interest: An interest in property restricted in its enjoyment to the owner personally cannot be transferred by him. For example, a right to sit in a particular chair in a temple or a right of personal residence cannot be transferred.

(e) Right to future maintenance: A right to future maintenance, in whatsoever manner arising, secured or determined, cannot be transferred. However, arrears of maintenance can be transferred as they have already accrued. The purpose is to protect the person entitled to maintenance from destitution.

(f) Mere right to sue: A mere right to sue cannot be transferred. For example, if A has a right to sue B for damages for breach of contract, A cannot transfer that right to C. However, if the right to sue is incidental to property, it can be transferred along with the property.

(g) Public office: No transfer can be made of a public office, nor of the salary of a public officer, whether before or after it has become payable.

(h) Pensions: Stipends allowed to military, naval, air-force, and civil pensioners of the government cannot be transferred.

(i) Nature contrary to interest: No transfer can be made of an interest which is by its nature not alienable, such as personal rights like the right to worship.

CASE LAW: Mulraj v. Murti Raghonathji Maharaj (1967) - SC held that the right to manage a temple is a restricted interest attached personally to the holder of the office and cannot be transferred to another person.

CASE LAW: Anand Rao v. Court of Wards - The transfer of spes successionis was held to be void and unenforceable.`,
