            title: `Q3. Explain the conditions restraining alienation and conditions restraining enjoyment. (Part B: 10m)`,
            content: `Condition Absolutely Restraining Alienation (Section 10)

Section 10 provides that where property is transferred subject to a condition or limitation absolutely restraining the transferee or any person claiming under him from parting with or disposing of his interest in the property, the condition or limitation is void. This means if A transfers a house to B on the condition that B shall never sell or mortgage it, the condition is void and B gets absolute ownership and can alienate the property. The rationale is that an absolute restraint on alienation is repugnant to the very nature of the estate granted. If you give someone full ownership but prevent them from transferring it, you are giving with one hand and taking away with the other.

Exceptions to Section 10: (1) A lease can contain a condition restraining the lessee from assigning or sub-letting the property, and such condition is valid. (2) A partial restraint on alienation is valid. For example, a condition that the transferee shall not sell to a particular person, or shall not sell for a particular period, or shall not sell without the consent of a particular person, may be valid if reasonable.

CASE LAW: Rosher v. Rosher (1884) - A condition that the transferee shall not sell without the consent of a particular person was held to be a partial restraint and valid.

CASE LAW: Bhagwan Dass v. Jiwan Lal - A condition that the property shall not be sold for 20 years was held to be not an absolute restraint but a partial restraint and was upheld as valid.

Condition Restraining Enjoyment (Section 11)

Section 11 provides that where, on a transfer of property, an interest therein is created absolutely in favour of any person, but the terms of the transfer direct that such interest shall be applied or enjoyed in a particular manner, he shall be entitled to receive and dispose of such interest as if there were no such direction. This means if A gives his house absolutely to B but directs that B shall use it only as a residence and not as a shop, the condition is void because it is repugnant to the absolute interest created. B can use it however he wishes.

The principle is: you cannot create a limited interest by imposing conditions that are repugnant to the nature of an absolute interest. If the condition limits the absolute interest created, it is void. However, if the interest itself is limited (such as a life estate), then conditions consistent with that limited interest are valid.

CASE LAW: Ramji Keshavji v. Ghusabhai - The court held that a condition restricting the mode of enjoyment of property that is inconsistent with the absolute nature of the interest created is void under Section 11.`,
