            title: `Q4. Explain Vested Interest and Contingent Interest with the help of illustrations. (Part A: 3m / Part B: 10m)`,
            content: `Vested Interest (Section 19)

A vested interest arises when the transfer creates an interest in favour of a person without specifying when it is to take effect, or when the interest is created to take effect on the happening of an event that must happen. The key characteristic is that the interest vests immediately even though enjoyment may be postponed. A vested interest is not defeated by the death of the transferee before obtaining possession.

Illustration: A transfers property to B for life and then to C. Here, C has a vested interest from the moment of the transfer. C's interest is vested because A's death is certain (an event that must happen). Even if C dies before A, C's heirs will get the property after A's death.

Another illustration: A promises to transfer his house to B on B's marriage. B is already engaged and will definitely get married. Since marriage is certain, B's interest is vested.

Contingent Interest (Section 21)

A contingent interest arises when the interest is created to take effect on the happening of a specified uncertain event. The interest does not vest until the condition is fulfilled. If the condition is not fulfilled, the interest fails completely.

Illustration: A transfers property to B if B passes the law examination. Here, B's interest is contingent because passing the examination is uncertain. If B passes, the interest vests. If B fails, the interest fails. Until B passes or fails, the interest remains contingent.

Another illustration: A transfers property to B for life and then to C if C survives B. Here, C's interest is contingent on surviving B. If C dies before B, the transfer to C fails.

Key Differences

1. Nature of event: In vested interest, the event is certain to happen; in contingent interest, the event is uncertain. 2. Transferability: Both are transferable, but a vested interest is more valuable. 3. Effect of death: Vested interest passes to heirs on death of the transferee; contingent interest may fail on death. 4. Heritable: Vested interest is always heritable; contingent interest is heritable only if the contingency is not personal survival. 5. Present right: Vested interest gives a present right of future enjoyment; contingent interest gives no present right.

CASE LAW: Gyan Chand v. Kamla Devi - SC held that the test for determining whether an interest is vested or contingent is the intention of the parties and the language of the instrument creating the interest.

CASE LAW: Rajes Kanta Roy v. Santi Debi (1957) - SC laid down that if the condition on which the interest depends must happen, the interest is vested; if it may or may not happen, it is contingent.`,
