            title: `Q5. Explain the Doctrine of Lis Pendens (Section 52). (Part A: 3m)`,
            content: `Section 52 embodies the doctrine of lis pendens, which means 'pending litigation'. During the pendency of any suit or proceeding in a court of competent jurisdiction, in which any right to immovable property is directly and specifically in question, the property cannot be transferred or otherwise dealt with by any party to the suit so as to affect the rights of any other party under any decree or order which may be made in the suit.

The principle underlying this doctrine is: it is necessary for the administration of justice that the decision of the court in a pending suit should be binding not only on the litigants but also on those who derive title from them during the pendency of the suit. Otherwise, a party could defeat the decree by transferring the property to a stranger.

Essentials of Lis Pendens: (1) A suit or proceeding must be pending in a court of competent jurisdiction, (2) The suit must not be collusive, (3) The right to immovable property must be directly and specifically in question, (4) The property must be transferred or dealt with by any party to the suit, (5) The transfer must affect the rights of the other party.

Effect: The transfer is not void; it is valid. But the transferee takes the property subject to the outcome of the suit. The transferee is bound by the decree. If the transferor loses the suit, the transferee also loses.

CASE LAW: Bellamy v. Sabine (1857) - The English court explained that lis pendens is not based on the doctrine of notice but on the principle that it is necessary for the orderly administration of justice that the decree should bind not only the parties but also those who derive title from them during the suit.

CASE LAW: Jayaram Mudaliar v. Ayyaswami (1972) - SC held that Section 52 applies to all transfers during pendency of suit, whether the transferee has notice of the suit or not. The transferee takes subject to the result of the litigation.

CASE LAW: Faiyaz Hussain Khan v. Prag Narain (1907) - Privy Council held that a lis pendens purchaser cannot be added as a necessary party to the suit; they are bound by the decree as they stand in the shoes of the transferor.`,
