            title: `Q6. Explain the Doctrine of Election (Section 35). (Part A: 3m)`,
            content: `Section 35 of the TPA provides for the doctrine of election. Election means choosing between two inconsistent or alternative rights. Where a person professes to transfer property which he has no right to transfer, and as part of the same transaction confers any benefit on the owner of that property, the owner must elect (choose) between accepting the benefit and retaining the property, or keeping the property and refusing the benefit.

Illustration: A has a house. B, by a single instrument, transfers A's house to C and gives Rs. 5 lakhs to A. Here, A must elect. Either A can accept the Rs. 5 lakhs and allow the transfer of the house to C, or A can retain the house but must then give up the benefit of Rs. 5 lakhs. A cannot keep both the house and the Rs. 5 lakhs.

Principles: (1) The person must be put to election between two inconsistent rights, (2) The person cannot approbate and reprobate (cannot accept and reject parts of the same transaction), (3) If the person accepts the benefit, they are deemed to have confirmed the transfer, (4) If they refuse, they must compensate the disappointed transferee.

CASE LAW: Dhanpat v. Gokul - The principle of election was applied where a Hindu widow purported to sell her deceased husband's property and gave a gift to his brother from the sale proceeds. The brother had to elect between accepting the gift and keeping the property.`,
