            title: `Q12. Define Lease. What are the different kinds of lease? Explain the rights and duties of lessor and lessee. (Part B: 10m)`,
            content: `Definition (Section 105)

A lease of immovable property is a transfer of a right to enjoy such property, made for a certain time, express or implied, or in perpetuity, in consideration of a price paid or promised, or of money, a share of crops, service, or any other thing of value, to be rendered periodically or on specified occasions to the transferor by the transferee, who accepts the transfer on such terms. The transferor is called the lessor, the transferee is called the lessee, the price is called the premium, and the money, share, service, or other thing to be rendered is called the rent.

Key distinction from sale: In a sale, ownership is transferred. In a lease, only the right to enjoy the property is transferred. The ownership remains with the lessor.

Essentials of a Valid Lease

(1) Parties: Lessor and lessee, both must be competent, (2) Subject matter: Immovable property, (3) Duration: Must be for a certain time or in perpetuity, (4) Consideration: Rent or premium, (5) Transfer of right to enjoy: Not ownership, (6) Acceptance: Lessee must accept.

Kinds of Lease

(1) Lease for a fixed period: Has a definite beginning and end. Terminates automatically on expiry. (2) Periodic lease (year to year, month to month): Renews automatically at the end of each period unless terminated by notice. (3) Lease in perpetuity: Continues forever, subject to payment of rent. (4) Tenancy at will (Section 106): Can be terminated at the will of either party by giving notice. Notice period for immovable property used for agricultural or manufacturing purposes is 6 months; for other purposes, 15 days. (5) Tenancy by holding over (Section 116): When a lessee remains in possession after the expiry of the lease term and the lessor accepts rent, a new lease is implied on the same terms as the old lease (except duration). (6) Tenancy by sufferance: When a tenant continues in possession without the consent of the landlord after the termination of the lease. The tenant has no legal right to possession.

Rights and Duties of Lessor (Section 108)

Duties: (a) Disclose material defects in the property known to the lessor but not likely to be discovered by the lessee with ordinary care, (b) Put the lessee in possession, (c) Allow the lessee to use and enjoy the property peacefully without interruption (covenant for quiet enjoyment). Rights: (a) Right to receive rent, (b) Right to re-enter on breach of conditions, (c) Right to have the property used reasonably, (d) Right to get back possession on termination.

Rights and Duties of Lessee (Section 108)

Duties: (a) Pay the rent, (b) Maintain the property in good condition (not commit waste), (c) Give notice of encroachments or threatened proceedings, (d) Use the property as a person of ordinary prudence would, (e) Not erect permanent structures without lessor's consent, (f) Not use the property for unlawful purposes, (g) Restore possession on termination, (h) Give notice of intended vacation. Rights: (a) Right to enjoy the property, (b) Right to make improvements and remove fixtures, (c) Right to crops sown before termination, (d) Right to assign or sub-let (unless restricted), (e) Right to deduct cost of repairs from rent if lessor fails to repair.

CASE LAW: Raj Kumar v. Firm Nemi Chand (1981) - SC held that the purpose of the lease must be specified and the lease is governed by the terms agreed upon by the parties, subject to the provisions of the TPA.`,
