            title: `Q13. Define Easement. What are its essentials? Discuss various kinds of easement. Explain acquisition and termination. Distinguish between Easement and Licence. (Part A: 3m / Part B: 10m)`,
            content: `Definition (Section 4, Indian Easements Act, 1882)

An easement is a right which the owner or occupier of certain land possesses, as such, for the beneficial enjoyment of that land, to do and continue to do something, or to prevent and continue to prevent something being done, in or upon, or in respect of, certain other land not his own. The land for whose benefit the easement exists is called the dominant heritage. Its owner or occupier is called the dominant owner. The land on which the liability is imposed is called the servient heritage. Its owner or occupier is called the servient owner.

Essentials of an Easement

(1) There must be a dominant heritage and a servient heritage: The two must be different properties. An easement cannot exist over one's own land. (2) The easement must accommodate the dominant heritage: It must benefit the land, not just the owner personally. A right that benefits a person personally (irrespective of ownership of land) is a licence, not an easement. (3) The dominant and servient owners must be different persons. (4) The right must be for the beneficial enjoyment of the dominant heritage: The right must have some connection with the enjoyment of the dominant heritage. (5) It must be a right to do something or to prevent something: It is either a positive right (to do something on the servient land) or a negative right (to prevent the servient owner from doing something).

Kinds of Easement

(1) Continuous and Discontinuous (Section 5): A continuous easement is one whose enjoyment is, or may be, continual without the act of man (e.g., right of light, right of support, right to receive air). A discontinuous easement is one that can be enjoyed only by the act of man (e.g., right of way, right to draw water from a well). The distinction is important for acquisition by prescription.

(2) Apparent and Non-apparent (Section 5): An apparent easement is one whose existence is shown by some permanent sign that would be discoverable on careful inspection (e.g., a beaten path, a window, a drain). A non-apparent easement has no such visible sign (e.g., a right to prevent a neighbour from building beyond a certain height). This distinction is also important for prescription and implied grant.

(3) Positive and Negative: A positive easement entitles the dominant owner to do something on the servient heritage (e.g., right of way, right to drain water). A negative easement entitles the dominant owner to prevent the servient owner from doing something on the servient heritage (e.g., right to light prevents construction that would block light).

Acquisition of Easement

(1) By Grant (Sections 7-12): Express grant through a deed. An easement may also arise by implied grant (e.g., when a person sells part of his land, the buyer gets an implied easement of necessity). (2) By Prescription (Section 15): By peaceful, open, and uninterrupted enjoyment for 20 years (30 years against government). The enjoyment must be as of right, not by permission, secrecy, or force. The 20-year period must be continuous. (3) By Necessity: When a person sells land that is landlocked, the buyer gets an easement of way of necessity over the seller's remaining land. (4) By Custom: Customary easements recognized by local usage.

Termination/Extinguishment (Sections 37-47)

An easement is extinguished by: (1) Dissolution of the dominant heritage (Sec. 37), (2) Expiry of time or happening of event (Sec. 38), (3) Release by the dominant owner (Sec. 39) - must be in writing if the easement was created by an instrument, (4) Revocation (Sec. 41), (5) Unity of ownership / Merger (Sec. 42) - when both heritages come under the same ownership, (6) Destruction of either heritage (Sec. 45), (7) Abandonment/Non-user - if the dominant owner does not exercise the right for 20 years, the easement is extinguished, (8) By decree of court, (9) By permanent change in character of the dominant heritage making the easement unnecessary.

Easement vs Licence (Section 52)

Licence (Section 52): Where one person grants to another, or to a definite number of other persons, a right to do, or continue to do, in or upon the immovable property of the grantor, something which would, in the absence of such right, be unlawful, and such right does not amount to an easement or an interest in the property, the right is called a licence.

Key Differences: (1) Nature: Easement is a right in rem (enforceable against the world); licence is a right in personam (personal permission). (2) Transferability: Easement runs with the land and is transferable with the dominant heritage; licence is personal and not transferable. (3) Revocability: Easement cannot be revoked at will; licence is generally revocable (with exceptions). (4) Two tenements: Easement requires two separate tenements; licence does not. (5) Registration: Easement may require registration if created by instrument; licence generally does not. (6) Heritable: Easement is heritable; licence dies with the licensee.

CASE LAW: Radhakisan Laxminarayan Toshniwal v. Shridhar Ramchandra Alshi (1960) - SC distinguished between an easement and a licence, holding that the critical test is whether the right benefits the land (easement) or benefits the person (licence).

CASE LAW: Associated Hotels of India v. R.N. Kapoor (1959) - SC held that a licence is a permission to do something that would otherwise be unlawful, and it does not create any interest in the property.

EXAM TIP: COMMON PART C PROBLEM: 'A person has been using a path across his neighbour's land for 25 years. Can he claim an easement?' ANSWER: Yes. Under Section 15 of the Indian Easements Act, if the enjoyment has been peaceful, open, and as of right (not by permission, secrecy, or force) for 20 years, the person acquires an easement by prescription. Since 25 years exceeds the statutory period, the right is established. However, the claimant must prove all the conditions - peaceful, open, continuous, and as of right.`,
