            title: `Problem 1: Transfer of Spes Successionis`,
            content: `Problem: A son who has a chance of inheriting a part of his father's property would like to transfer such property during the lifetime of his father. Can he succeed? Discuss.

Answer:

Issue: Whether a person can transfer property that he expects to inherit during the lifetime of the property owner.

Rule: Section 6(a) of the Transfer of Property Act provides that the chance of an heir-apparent succeeding to an estate, the chance of a relation obtaining a legacy on the death of a kinsman, or any other mere possibility of a like nature, cannot be transferred. This is known as spes successionis or mere expectancy of succession.

Application: In the present case, the son has only an expectation of inheriting his father's property. During the father's lifetime, the son has no present right, title, or interest in the property. The father may dispose of the property during his lifetime, or may disinherit the son by will, or may outlive the son. Therefore, the son's interest is a mere possibility, not a present right, and falls squarely within the prohibition of Section 6(a).

Conclusion: The son cannot transfer the property. Any such transfer would be void under Section 6(a) of the TPA. As held by the Privy Council in Syed Mohd. Baksh v. Fakhr Jahan, spes successionis is not transferable as it is a mere possibility.`,
