            title: `Q6. Explain Shares, Share Capital, and Debentures. Distinguish between Shares and Debentures. (Part A: 3m / Part B: 10m)`,
            content: `Shares

A share is the interest of a shareholder in a company, measured by a sum of money, for the purpose of liability in the first place and of interest in the second. Under Section 2(84), share means a share in the share capital of a company. Types: (1) Equity shares - carry voting rights, right to dividend (not guaranteed), right to surplus on winding up. (2) Preference shares - carry preferential right to dividend at a fixed rate and preferential right to return of capital on winding up.

Share Capital

Types: (1) Authorized/Nominal capital - maximum amount the company can raise as stated in MoA. (2) Issued capital - portion of authorized capital offered to public. (3) Subscribed capital - portion of issued capital actually subscribed by public. (4) Paid-up capital - amount actually paid by shareholders. (5) Called-up capital - portion of subscribed capital called upon by the company.

Debentures

A debenture is a document acknowledging a debt owed by the company. Under Section 2(30), debenture includes debenture stock, bonds, and any other instrument evidencing a debt. Types: (1) Secured/Mortgage debentures - secured by a charge on company assets. (2) Unsecured/Naked debentures. (3) Registered debentures - transferred by transfer deed. (4) Bearer debentures - transferable by delivery. (5) Redeemable debentures - repayable on a specified date. (6) Convertible debentures - can be converted to shares. (7) Fixed charge - charge on specific assets. (8) Floating charge - charge on a class of assets that crystallizes on default.

Distinction: Shares vs Debentures

(1) Nature: Shares = ownership capital; Debentures = borrowed capital/debt. (2) Holder: Shareholder = member/owner; Debenture holder = creditor. (3) Return: Dividend (from profits only, not guaranteed); Interest (fixed, paid regardless of profits). (4) Voting: Shareholders have voting rights; debenture holders generally do not. (5) Repayment: Shares are not repayable during company's lifetime (except buy-back); debentures are redeemable. (6) Security: Shares are not secured; debentures may be secured by charge. (7) Risk: Higher for shareholders; lower for debenture holders. (8) Winding up: Debenture holders paid before shareholders.`,
