            title: `Q7. Explain the Winding Up of a Company. Discuss the types, grounds, and role of liquidator. (Part B: 10m)`,
            content: `Definition

Winding up is the process by which the life of a company is brought to an end. It involves realizing the company's assets, paying off its debts and liabilities, and distributing any remaining surplus among the members. After winding up, the company is dissolved and ceases to exist.

Types of Winding Up

(1) Winding Up by the Tribunal (Compulsory Winding Up - Sec. 271-274): The National Company Law Tribunal (NCLT) may order winding up on the following grounds: (a) If the company has resolved by special resolution to be wound up, (b) If the company has acted against sovereignty or integrity of India or is against friendly relations with foreign states, (c) If the affairs of the company have been conducted in a fraudulent manner or the company was formed for fraudulent or unlawful purpose, (d) If the company has made a default in filing financial statements or annual returns for five consecutive financial years, (e) If the Tribunal is of the opinion that it is just and equitable that the company should be wound up.

(2) Voluntary Winding Up (Sec. 304-323): (a) Members' voluntary winding up - the directors make a declaration of solvency (that the company can pay its debts in full within 3 years). The members pass a special resolution. The members appoint a liquidator. (b) Creditors' voluntary winding up - when the directors cannot make a declaration of solvency. The creditors also participate in appointing the liquidator.

Role and Powers of Liquidator

The Official Liquidator or the appointed liquidator: (a) Takes into custody and control all property, effects, and actionable claims to which the company is entitled, (b) Settles the list of contributories, (c) Makes calls on contributories, (d) Realizes the assets and distributes proceeds, (e) Can institute or defend legal proceedings on behalf of the company, (f) Can carry on the business of the company so far as necessary for beneficial winding up, (g) Can sell the property, (h) Can compromise claims.

Order of Payment

(1) Costs of winding up, (2) Preferential creditors (employees' wages for 4 months, holiday wages, government taxes due for 12 months), (3) Secured creditors (from the proceeds of charged assets), (4) Unsecured creditors, (5) Preference shareholders, (6) Equity shareholders.

CASE LAW: Ebrahimi v. Westbourne Galleries Ltd. (1973) - The House of Lords discussed the 'just and equitable' ground for winding up and held that it includes situations of breach of understanding between members, exclusion from management, and loss of confidence. The court looks at the underlying obligations and not just the strict legal rights.`,
