            title: `Q1. Explain the evolution, nature, and scope of Administrative Law. Why has it grown? (Part B: 10m)`,
            content: `Definition

Administrative Law is the body of law that governs the activities of administrative agencies of government. It deals with the powers and procedures of administrative agencies, including the law governing judicial review of administrative action. According to Ivor Jennings, Administrative Law is the law relating to the administration. It determines the organization, powers, and duties of administrative authorities. K.C. Davis defined it as the law concerning the powers and procedures of administrative agencies, including especially the law governing judicial review of administrative action.

Reasons for Growth of Administrative Law

(1) Change from Laissez Faire to Welfare State: The modern state has assumed enormous responsibilities for the welfare of its citizens - education, health, housing, employment, social security. These require vast administrative machinery. (2) Inadequacy of Legislative Process: Parliament cannot deal with every detail of governance. It lays down broad policies and delegates the details to administrative agencies. (3) Inadequacy of Judicial System: Courts are overburdened and cannot handle the volume of disputes arising from modern governance. Administrative tribunals provide quicker, cheaper, and more specialized justice. (4) Flexibility: Administrative agencies can act more quickly and flexibly than the legislature or judiciary. Rules and regulations can be modified quickly. (5) Technical Expertise: Many modern problems (telecommunications, aviation, nuclear energy) require specialized technical knowledge that legislators and judges may not possess. (6) Preventive Action: Administrative agencies can take preventive measures (licensing, inspection) rather than waiting for disputes to arise. (7) Experimentation: Administrative agencies can experiment with different approaches and modify them based on experience.

Relationship between Constitutional Law and Administrative Law

Constitutional Law deals with the structure of the State and its organs, fundamental rights, and the distribution of powers. Administrative Law deals with the powers, procedures, and control of administrative agencies. They are interrelated: Constitutional Law is the genus, Administrative Law is the species. Constitutional Law provides the framework; Administrative Law fills in the details. As Ivor Jennings said, Administrative Law is the law about the powers and the procedure of the organs of government.`,
