            title: `Q1. Define Construction and Interpretation. Distinguish between them. Explain the general principles. (Part B: 10m)`,
            content: `Interpretation vs Construction

Interpretation means the art of finding out the true sense of an enactment by giving the words their natural and ordinary meaning. It is the process of ascertaining the meaning of the words used by the legislature. Construction goes beyond the words. It is the process of drawing conclusions about subjects that lie beyond the direct expression of the text, from elements known from and given in the text. In other words, interpretation finds meaning IN the words; construction finds meaning BEYOND the words.

Practical distinction: When the language of a statute is clear and unambiguous, the court merely interprets (gives the plain meaning). When the language is ambiguous or leads to absurdity, the court constructs (determines the legislative intent by looking at context, purpose, and other aids). In practice, the terms are often used interchangeably.

General Principles of Interpretation

(1) Primary Rule - Literal/Grammatical Rule: The starting point is always the plain meaning. Words must be given their natural, ordinary, and grammatical meaning. If the language is clear, the court must give effect to it regardless of consequences. The court cannot add to or subtract from the words of the statute. As stated by the Supreme Court: when the words of a statute are clear, there is no room for interpretation.

CASE LAW: Sussex Peerage Case (1844) - The House of Lords held that the duty of the court is to interpret the words that the legislature has used. If the words are clear, the court must give effect to them, whether or not the result is sensible.

(2) Golden Rule: A modification of the literal rule. Words are given their ordinary meaning, but if the literal interpretation leads to absurdity, repugnancy, or inconsistency, the court may modify the meaning to the extent necessary to avoid the absurd result, while remaining as close as possible to the literal meaning.

CASE LAW: Grey v. Pearson (1857) - Lord Wensleydale stated the golden rule: the grammatical and ordinary sense of the words is to be adhered to, unless that would lead to some absurdity or inconsistency with the rest of the instrument, in which case the grammatical and ordinary sense of the words may be modified, so as to avoid that absurdity and inconsistency, but no farther.

(3) Mischief Rule (Rule in Heydon's Case): The most flexible and purposive rule. The court examines: (i) What was the common law before the Act? (ii) What was the mischief or defect that the common law did not address? (iii) What remedy has Parliament prescribed? (iv) What is the true reason for the remedy? The court then adopts the construction that suppresses the mischief and advances the remedy.

CASE LAW: Heydon's Case (1584) - The oldest and most important case on interpretation. The Barons of the Exchequer laid down the four questions that must be considered (stated above). This rule directs the court to look at the purpose of the legislation and give an interpretation that fulfils that purpose.

CASE LAW: Bengal Immunity Co. v. State of Bihar (1955) - SC applied the mischief rule to interpret the scope of Article 286 of the Constitution, looking at what mischief the provision was designed to remedy.

(4) Harmonious Construction: When two provisions of the same statute appear to be in conflict, the court should try to give effect to both by interpreting them harmoniously. Neither provision should be rendered redundant or otiose. Each provision should be given its full effect.

CASE LAW: CIT v. Hindustan Bulk Carriers (2003) - SC laid down 5 principles of harmonious construction: (i) Courts must avoid a head-on clash between two sections and must construe them so that both survive. (ii) A provision cannot be used to defeat another unless the court finds it impossible to reconcile them. (iii) When reconciliation is impossible, the court must decide which provision is leading and which is subordinate. (iv) One provision cannot be used to nullify another. (v) The court must give effect to both provisions.

CASE LAW: Venkataramana Devaru v. State of Mysore (1958) - SC harmoniously interpreted Article 25(2)(b) (temple entry for all Hindus) and Article 26(b) (right of religious denomination to manage its affairs). The court held that the right to exclude non-members from the inner sanctum could be preserved while allowing public access to the outer parts.`,
