            title: `Q3. Explain the Doctrine of Pith and Substance. (Part A: 3m / Part B: 10m)`,
            content: `The Doctrine of Pith and Substance is a principle of constitutional interpretation used in federal systems to determine whether a particular legislation falls within the competence of the legislature that enacted it. The court looks at the true nature, character, and subject matter of the legislation (its pith and substance) rather than its form or incidental effects.

In India, legislative powers are distributed between Parliament (Union List - List I) and State Legislatures (State List - List II), with concurrent powers in List III (Seventh Schedule, Article 246). When a law enacted by one legislature appears to encroach on the domain of another, the court applies the pith and substance test.

The test: What is the dominant or primary purpose of the legislation? If, in its true nature and character, the legislation falls within the competence of the enacting legislature, it is valid even if it incidentally encroaches upon matters in the other list. The incidental encroachment does not invalidate the legislation.

CASE LAW: State of Bombay v. F.N. Balsara (1951) - The Bombay Prohibition Act prohibited the sale and consumption of liquor. It was challenged as encroaching on the Union's power over import/export (Union List). The SC applied the pith and substance test and held that the Act, in its true nature, was about public health and order (State List). The incidental effect on import/export did not make it unconstitutional.

CASE LAW: Prafulla Kumar Mukherjee v. Bank of Commerce (1947) - The Privy Council applied the pith and substance doctrine and held that the Bengal Money Lenders Act was in pith and substance about money lending (a State subject), even though it incidentally affected promissory notes (a Union subject).

CASE LAW: Kartar Singh v. State of Punjab (1994) - SC applied pith and substance to uphold TADA (Terrorist and Disruptive Activities Act), holding that in its pith and substance, it related to public order and terrorism, even though it had some provisions that touched upon law and order (typically a State subject).`,
