            title: `Q4. What is Judicial Restraint and Judicial Activism? Explain with landmark cases. (Part A: 3m / Part B: 10m)`,
            content: `Judicial Restraint

Judicial Restraint is the philosophy that judges should exercise self-limitation and refrain from interfering with legislative and executive policy decisions. Judges should interpret the law as written and defer to the elected branches of government on policy matters. The rationale is based on: (1) Separation of powers - the judiciary should not encroach on the domain of the legislature or executive, (2) Democratic legitimacy - judges are not elected and should not substitute their policy preferences for those of elected representatives, (3) Institutional competence - courts are not equipped to make policy decisions that require expertise in governance, economics, or social planning.

Judicial Activism

Judicial Activism is the philosophy that courts should take an active role in protecting rights and ensuring justice, even if this means creative interpretation of the law, issuing directions to the executive, or filling legislative gaps. In India, judicial activism has been particularly prominent through:

(1) Public Interest Litigation (PIL): The SC relaxed the traditional requirement of locus standi (only the aggrieved person can file a petition) to allow any public-spirited person to approach the court on behalf of the disadvantaged or oppressed.

(2) Expansive interpretation of Article 21: The SC has read into the right to life and personal liberty an entire range of rights not explicitly mentioned in the Constitution: right to livelihood, right to education, right to clean environment, right to health, right to shelter, right to privacy, right to fair trial, right to speedy trial, right to legal aid, right against custodial torture, right to live with dignity.

(3) Filling legislative gaps: In cases where the legislature has failed to act, the SC has laid down guidelines that have the force of law until the legislature enacts proper legislation.

CASE LAW: Vishakha v. State of Rajasthan (1997) - In the absence of legislation on sexual harassment at the workplace, the SC laid down comprehensive guidelines (Vishakha Guidelines) that had the force of law until the Sexual Harassment of Women at Workplace Act was passed in 2013. This is a classic example of judicial activism filling a legislative vacuum.

CASE LAW: MC Mehta v. Union of India (various cases) - SC took suo motu cognizance of environmental pollution and directed closure of polluting industries, relocation of hazardous industries, and various measures for environmental protection. The court developed the principle of absolute liability (going beyond strict liability) for hazardous industries.

CASE LAW: Hussainara Khatoon v. State of Bihar (1979) - SC discovered that thousands of undertrial prisoners had been languishing in jail for periods longer than the maximum punishment for their alleged offences. The court directed their release and established the right to speedy trial and free legal aid as part of Article 21.

CASE LAW: Olga Tellis v. Bombay Municipal Corporation (1985) - SC held that the right to livelihood is part of the right to life under Article 21. Pavement dwellers cannot be evicted without following due process. This expanded Article 21 significantly.

CASE LAW: Antulay Case (1988) - SC recognized the right to speedy trial as a fundamental right under Article 21 and laid down guidelines for ensuring timely disposal of cases.

Criticism of Judicial Activism: (1) Undermines separation of powers, (2) Courts lack expertise in policy-making, (3) Judicial legislation without democratic accountability, (4) Overburdening the judiciary, (5) Selective activism (courts choose which cases to be active in).`,
