            title: `Q6. Strict Construction is adopted for Penal and Fiscal Statutes. Comment. (Part B: 10m)`,
            content: `Strict Construction of Penal Statutes

Penal statutes (statutes that create offences and impose punishments) must be strictly construed in favour of the accused. This means: (1) If the words of a penal statute are capable of two interpretations, the interpretation favourable to the accused must be adopted. (2) The court cannot extend the scope of a penal statute by analogy, implication, or intendment. (3) The court cannot create an offence by stretching the language. (4) Every ingredient of the offence must be clearly established.

The rationale is the protection of personal liberty. Since penal statutes affect the life and liberty of individuals, they must be clear and certain. Citizens must be able to know in advance what conduct is prohibited. The State must bring the case clearly within the letter of the law.

Strict Construction of Fiscal/Taxation Statutes

Fiscal statutes (taxing statutes) are also strictly construed. The principle is: no tax can be imposed without clear authority of law. There is no room for intendment or equity in taxation. The taxing authority must show that the case falls strictly within the charging provision. If there is any ambiguity in the charging section, the benefit goes to the taxpayer/assessee.

CASE LAW: CIT v. Vegetable Products Ltd. (1973) - SC held that if two reasonable constructions of a taxing statute are possible, the one favouring the assessee must be adopted. The subject is not to be taxed without clear words for that purpose.

CASE LAW: Commissioner of Sales Tax v. Modi Sugar Mills (1961) - SC held that the court must look at what is clearly expressed in a taxing statute. There is no equity about tax. Nothing is to be read in, nothing is to be implied. One can look only at the language used.

CASE LAW: Tolaram Relumal v. State of Bombay (1954) - SC applied strict construction to a penal provision and held that where the words of a statute are clear and unambiguous, they must be followed even if the result may seem harsh or unreasonable.

However, strict construction does not mean narrowest possible construction. The court must give the words their natural meaning while being careful not to stretch them beyond what they can reasonably bear. Also, exemption provisions in fiscal statutes should be construed liberally in favour of the assessee.`,
