            title: `Q8. Explain the Doctrine of Repugnancy. When does a State law become void? (Part A: 3m)`,
            content: `Under Article 254 of the Constitution, when a State law is repugnant to a Central law on a subject in the Concurrent List, the Central law prevails and the State law is void to the extent of the repugnancy. However, if the State law has received the President's assent, it prevails in that State over the Central law.

Tests for repugnancy (as laid down by the SC): (1) Direct conflict: Where there is a direct conflict between the provisions of the two laws, making it impossible to obey both. (2) Same field: Where both laws occupy the same field and the Central law is intended to be exhaustive or complete. (3) Implicit inconsistency: Where the Central law, though not directly conflicting, covers the field so completely that no room is left for the State law to operate.

CASE LAW: Deep Chand v. State of UP (1959) - SC laid down the three tests for determining repugnancy between Central and State laws.

CASE LAW: M. Karunanidhi v. Union of India (1979) - SC elaborated on the doctrine and held that repugnancy can arise: (i) when there is direct inconsistency between the two enactments, (ii) when Parliament intends to occupy the entire field, and (iii) when Central law is a complete code on a subject.`,
