            content: `Problem: A Law was made that smoking in public is an offence from 1.4.2023. A person is prosecuted for smoking in public on 31.3.2023. Is the prosecution valid?

Answer:

Issue: Whether a person can be prosecuted under a law that came into force after the act was committed.

Rule: Article 20(1) of the Constitution is a fundamental right that prohibits retrospective criminal legislation. No person shall be convicted of any offence except for violation of a law in force at the time of the commission of the act charged as an offence. This protection is absolute and cannot be suspended even during Emergency.

Application: The act of smoking in public was committed on 31.3.2023. The law making it an offence came into force on 1.4.2023. At the time the act was committed, it was not an offence. Applying Article 20(1), the person cannot be prosecuted for an act that was not illegal when it was done.

Conclusion: The prosecution is NOT valid. It is unconstitutional under Article 20(1). The fundamental principle of criminal law is: nullum crimen sine lege (no crime without law). A person can only be punished for violating a law that existed at the time of the act.`,
